
✅✅✅✅✅✅✅✅✅✅ Microservices ✅✅✅✅✅✅✅✅✅✅
A monolith keeps all modules inside a single application.
Orders + Payments + Notifications + Auth
run and deploy together.

Advantages
Single deployment
Easier local development and debugging
Simple communication between modules (function calls)
Easier transactions and consistency
Lower operational complexity
Easier monitoring/logging initially

Internal calls are simple:
function calls
instead of network calls.

Drawbacks of Monoliths
1. Deployment Dependencies
Even a small change requires redeploying the entire application.

2. Scaling Problems
Suppose only the Orders module becomes heavy.
With monolith scaling:
Orders + Payments + Notifications
all scale together even if only Orders needs more resources.
This wastes CPU and memory.

3. Tech Stack Constraints
Different workloads may require different technologies.

Example:
multithreading → Go
CSV/Excel processing → Python
Monolith forces everything into one stack.

======================== Drawbacks of Microservices ========================
1. Network Overhead
Internal function calls become network calls.
Orders Service -> Payments Service

Now you have:
HTTP/gRPC latency
retries
timeouts
partial failures

A simple function call becomes distributed communication.

2. Distributed System Complexity
Load Balancer
-> Routing Layer
-> Orders Service
-> Payments Service
-> Notification Service

Debugging becomes difficult because requests pass through multiple services.

3. Data Inconsistency
Each service usually owns its own database.
Orders DB
Payments DB
Users DB
Cross-service transactions become difficult.

Problems:
eventual consistency
partial failures
duplicate events
distributed transaction complexity

Payment succeeds
Order update fails

✅✅✅✅✅✅✅✅✅✅ Serverless Architecture ✅✅✅✅✅✅✅✅✅✅
In traditional infrastructure, we manually decide:
CPU
RAM
machine size
8 GB RAM, 2 CPUs

Choosing the correct machine size is difficult because traffic changes over time.

Normal traffic vs sale traffic
Auto Scaling
One solution is auto scaling.

Problem
CPU > 70% -> start new server
Auto scaling is reactive.
Starting a new server takes time:

VM/container boot
OS startup
loading application code
dependency initialization

During sudden traffic spikes, requests may fail before new servers become ready.

======================== Serverless ========================

In serverless architecture:
developers only provide code
cloud provider manages servers and infrastructure

Code is deployed as:
Functions
Functions run when triggered by events:

HTTP request
queue message
file upload
cron job

You pay only for:
execution/processing time

No need to manage:
servers
OS
scaling
provisioning

Common platforms:
Amazon Web Services Lambda
Cloudflare Workers
Google Cloud Functions

1. Cold Starts
If no function instance is running, the platform must:
start runtime/container/VM
load code
initialize dependencies

This delay is called:
Cold Start
Optimization

Modern platforms reduce startup time using:
lightweight VMs
V8 isolates
prewarmed containers

Example:
Cloudflare Workers uses V8 isolates with extremely low startup time.

Language Impact
Languages like:
JavaScript
Python
usually have lower cold starts because startup is lightweight.

Languages like:
Java
.NET
often have slower cold starts due to heavier runtime initialization (JVM, CLR).

2. Execution Limits
Serverless functions usually have execution time limits.

AWS Lambda max execution time:
15 minutes

Not suitable for:
long-running processes
persistent workers

3. Stateless Nature
Serverless functions are designed to be stateless.
Problems with:
persistent DB connections
WebSockets
in-memory sessions
long-lived TCP state

Each invocation should ideally be independent.

Excellent for:
image/video processing
file conversion
queue consumers
cron jobs
event-driven workloads

Example:
Upload video -> trigger Lambda -> process video

Especially useful for heavy workloads that run occasionally instead of continuously.